<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|<tuple|tmdoc|english>>

<\body>
  <tmdoc-title|Grammars and their tables>

  A grammar becomes parse tables once, in <scheme> and <c++>; the tables
  are kept as a text in a cache, and the C runtime of tree-sitter loads
  that text at the first use of the language. Nothing is compiled per
  grammar.

  <section|The generator>

  The modules of <source-link|progs/treesit|TeXmacs/progs/treesit> follow
  the crate <verbatim|generate> of tree-sitter 0.27.1, stage by stage,
  since the order of its operations shows in the tables (numbers of the
  states, choice among equal items):

  <\description>
    <item*|<source-link|ts-base.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-base.scm>>Symbols
    (integers ordered as the symbols of tree-sitter), token sets,
    character sets as sorted lists of ranges, growable vectors, a reader
    of <abbr|JSON>.

    <item*|<source-link|ts-grammar.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-grammar.scm>>Reading
    <verbatim|grammar.json> into rules, and the steps of
    <verbatim|prepare_grammar>: the tokens extracted into the lexical
    grammar, the repetitions as auxiliary rules, the productions, the
    default aliases, the inlined rules.

    <item*|<source-link|ts-nfa.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-nfa.scm>>The
    patterns of the tokens, parsed with the simplifications of the crate
    <verbatim|regex_syntax> (they decide the shape of the automaton), and
    the <abbr|NFA> of the tokens; the Unicode classes come from
    <source-link|ts-unicode.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-unicode.scm>,
    generated by <source-link|gen-unicode.py|misc/tree-sitter/gen-unicode.py>.

    <item*|<source-link|ts-tables.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-tables.scm>>The
    <abbr|LR(1)> item sets and the parse table, with the conflict
    resolution of tree-sitter.

    <item*|<source-link|ts-build.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-build.scm>>The
    token conflicts, the keywords, the error state, the merging of
    compatible states and the lex tables (<abbr|DFA>s).

    <item*|<source-link|ts-language.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-language.scm>,
    <source-link|ts-export.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-export.scm>>What
    <verbatim|render.rs> writes into <tt|parser.c>, as plain data (the
    symbols renumbered as the runtime numbers them, the alias and
    supertype maps of the queries), and the text of it which
    <source-link|ts_dynamic.c|src/Plugins/TreeSitter/ts_dynamic.c> reads
    (integers, and strings written <verbatim|LENGTH:BYTES>, starting with
    <verbatim|tsdyn1>).
  </description>

  In <scheme>, the generation of a large grammar takes minutes and
  gigabytes. When the glue <scm|tree-sitter-tables-start> exists,
  <source-link|ts-native.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-native.scm> sends the
  prepared syntax grammar to <c++> as a text;
  <source-link|ts_tables.cpp|src/Plugins/TreeSitter/ts_tables.cpp> builds
  the <abbr|LR(1)> states and the token conflicts and sends back what the
  choice of the keywords needs, which stays in <scheme>;
  <scm|tree-sitter-tables-finish> then merges the states and builds the
  lex tables, and <scm|tree-sitter-tables-export> writes the whole text, to
  which <scheme> only adds what depends on the symbols (their names,
  fields and aliases). The entry point is <scm|ts-generate-tsdyn> of
  <source-link|ts-export.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-export.scm>. The <c++> port gives the same text byte for
  byte, the <scheme> code being the reference: <scm|(ts-set-native-tables!
  #f)> or the variable <verbatim|TS_SCHEME_TABLES> select the <scheme>
  path, and the suite compares both. With <verbatim|TS_PROFILE=1> each
  stage prints its time (and, for the <c++> stages, the memory).

  <section|The registry and the cache>

  <source-link|ts-registry.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-registry.scm> knows
  the grammars by their names in
  <source-link|misc/tree-sitter|TeXmacs/misc/tree-sitter> (which are not
  always the names of the program languages: the language
  <verbatim|shell> has the grammar <verbatim|bash>):

  <\itemize>
    <item><scm|treesit-languages>, the grammars of the list of the program
    languages, and <scm|treesit-supported?>, true when the build has the
    glue and the grammar is there;

    <item><scm|treesit-cache-url>: the tables of a grammar are cached in
    <verbatim|$TEXMACS_HOME_PATH/system/cache/tree-sitter/\<less\>grammar\<gtr\>-\<less\>hash\<gtr\>.tsdyn>,
    the hash being that of the text of <verbatim|grammar.json> preceded by
    <scm|treesit-generator-version>. A changed grammar, or a new version
    of the generator, thus gives another file, and the old one is no
    longer read. The version is to be changed with the generator or the
    exported data;

    <item><scm|treesit-generate-cache> generates the tables and writes them
    under a name ending in <verbatim|.part>, then renames the file, so that
    a reader never sees half of it;

    <item><scm|treesit-language> gives the index of a grammar in the C
    runtime when its tables are ready, else <scm|#f>, and then starts their
    generation; <scm|treesit-prepare> calls a continuation when they are
    ready. The generation runs in a second <TeXmacs> without window
    (<verbatim|texmacs.bin -headless -x>, through
    <scm|async-evaluate-system>), with the message ``Preparing the syntax
    of ...'', while the usual highlighter colours the code.
  </itemize>

  The tables of all the grammars are shipped as well, compressed by xz, as
  <verbatim|misc/tree-sitter/\<less\>grammar\<gtr\>/\<less\>name of the
  cache file\<gtr\>.xz> (1.6 MB for the 22 grammars): generating them all
  at the first start would take more than a minute, Lean alone about a
  minute and several gigabytes. <scm|treesit-cached?> unpacks them into the
  cache when it lacks them (glue <scm|tree-sitter-unpack>,
  <source-link|ts_xz.c|src/Plugins/TreeSitter/ts_xz.c>). Since the name
  holds the hash, shipped tables of an older grammar or generator are
  ignored and the grammar is generated as before.
  <source-link|ship-tables.sh|misc/tree-sitter/ship-tables.sh> makes them
  again; the suite fails while they do not match.

  <section|From the tables to the runtime>

  <scm|tree-sitter-language-load> reads the cached file in <c++> (no
  <scheme> string of a megabyte) and gives it to <cpp|tsdyn_language_new>:
  every state becomes a large state (one row of the parse table), the lex
  functions interpret the lex tables, and since the runtime calls them
  without the language, each loaded language takes one of
  <cpp|TSDYN_MAX_LANGUAGES> (32) pairs of functions. The result is an
  index into the languages of
  <source-link|tree_sitter_glue.cpp|src/Plugins/TreeSitter/tree_sitter_glue.cpp>,
  which <scheme> passes to all the other glue routines. Queries are
  compiled by <scm|tree-sitter-query-new> for a language; their regular
  expressions (<verbatim|#match?>, and the Lua patterns of
  <verbatim|#lua-match?>) are compiled once with
  <verbatim|std::regex>.

  <section|External scanners in <scheme>>

  A grammar with a <verbatim|scanner.c> needs a port of it,
  <verbatim|ts-scanner-\<less\>grammar\<gtr\>.scm>, listed in
  <scm|treesit-scanners> of the registry. The protocol is described in
  <source-link|ts-scan.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-scan.scm>: a scanner is
  a vector <scm|#(create scan deserialize serialize)>; <scm|(scan payload
  lexer valid)>, the payload being what <scm|create> made, reads the text
  with <scm|lexer-lookahead>,
  <scm|lexer-advance!>, <scm|lexer-mark-end!>, <scm|lexer-set-result!>,
  <scm|lexer-column>, <scm|lexer-eof?>, <scm|valid> being the vector of
  the external tokens which the parser accepts. <scm|(serialize payload)>
  gives the state, a value compared by <scm|equal?> and never modified
  afterwards (<scm|#f> for the initial state), which <scm|(deserialize
  payload state)> puts back; <scm|record-scanner> makes these for a vector
  of fields. A scanner without
  state, as that of <abbr|TOML>, ends with:

  <\verbatim-code>
    (define-public (ts-toml-scanner)

    \ \ (stateless-scanner toml-scan))
  </verbatim-code>

  The same functions serve both parsers: on the vector lexer of
  <source-link|ts-parse.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-parse.scm> they act in <scheme>, on anything else they call
  the glue <scm|tree-sitter-lexer-...> on the current <cpp|TSLexer>.
  <scm|tree-sitter-set-scanner> installs <c++> trampolines (one create
  function per language, 32 slots, a language given its scanner again
  keeping its slot): the vectors of valid tokens are made once per row of
  the table of the external states, and the texts of the serialized
  states are mapped back to their values, so that the reader is rarely
  called. The loops over characters of <source-link|ts-scan.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-scan.scm>
  (<scm|scan-advance-while!>, <scm|scan-advance-until!>,
  <scm|scan-skip-spaces!>) run in C on the C lexer. The C code of a
  scanner is followed closely, quirks included, since the corpus of the
  grammar checks the trees.

  <section|Adding a language>

  <\enumerate>
    <item>A directory <verbatim|misc/tree-sitter/\<less\>grammar\<gtr\>> with
    <verbatim|grammar.json>, its <verbatim|LICENSE>, a <verbatim|SOURCE>
    which names the repository and its commit (and what is ours or
    patched), and the queries: <verbatim|highlights.scm> (a first line
    <verbatim|; inherits: c> puts the query of another grammar before it),
    and when there are some <verbatim|injections.scm>,
    <verbatim|tags.scm> (the definitions), <verbatim|indents.scm> (those of
    nvim-treesitter, Apache-2.0, the files they inherit put in front) and
    <verbatim|closers.scm> (made by
    <source-link|gen-closers.py|misc/tree-sitter/gen-closers.py>). There
    is no query of folds: folds come from the nodes of several lines.

    <item>An entry of
    <source-link|tm-program-languages.scm|TeXmacs/progs/kernel/texmacs/tm-program-languages.scm>
    with <scm|(grammar "...")>, and its comments
    (<scm|comment>, <scm|block-comment>) and other names (<scm|names>,
    also used to resolve injections). This list also says what a new
    language needs outside of tree-sitter: the macros of its tags, its
    format and its <verbatim|-lang.scm>.

    <item>The port of the scanner, if any, in <scm|treesit-scanners> and in
    the modules used by the registry; without <verbatim|indents.scm>, the
    rules of indentation of the language in <scm|syntax-indent-rules> of
    <source-link|ts-edit.scm|TeXmacs/progs/treesit/ts-edit.scm> (as for
    YAML, <LaTeX>, Lean and Haskell), else the lines are not indented by
    the tree. The runtime has 32 places for languages and 32 for scanners,
    shared by all (22 grammars and 17 scanners today).

    <item>The checks of
    <source-link|misc/tree-sitter|misc/tree-sitter>: <source-link|corpus.py|misc/tree-sitter/corpus.py>
    (the corpus of the grammar through the C runtime, compared as
    <verbatim|tree-sitter test> does), <source-link|highlights.py|misc/tree-sitter/highlights.py> (the
    captures compared with those of <verbatim|tree-sitter query>), then
    <source-link|ship-tables.sh|misc/tree-sitter/ship-tables.sh> for its tables. <source-link|check-grammars.py|misc/tree-sitter/check-grammars.py>
    compares the grammars with clones of their repositories and updates
    one.
  </enumerate>

